dator graph routes each query to Corrective RAG, NL-to-SQL, a human-in-the-loop email tool or chat.","url":"#p-agentic-rag","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/Multi-Agent-RAG"},{"id":"openenv","name":"OpenEnv Data-Cleaning Agent","kind":"Reinforcement learning environment","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"0.967","label":"PPO grader score on hard-clean"},"stack":["OpenEnv","Stable-Baselines3 PPO","Llama 3.1 8B","Pydantic","FastAPI","Docker","HF Spaces"],"summary":"Cleaning a messy table takes many small, ordered decisions. An OpenEnv environment over pandas tables with reproducible (task, seed) episodes and a hidden clean ground truth.","url":"#p-openenv","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/open-env-nuclei"},{"id":"sleep-llm","name":"Sleep-Consolidating LLM","kind":"Continual-learning research","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"0.325","label":"recall of earlier days' facts after 5 nights, no notebook (naive training: 0.035)"},"stack":["PyTorch","Qwen2.5-0.5B","QLoRA (NF4)","PEFT","bitsandbytes","Transformers","pytest"],"summary":"A language model learns nothing from what happens after training. Each simulated day a 4-bit Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct reads documents from a seeded synthetic world of names it cannot know (about 20% of facts are later updated) and writes atomic notes.","url":"#p-sleep-llm","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/Sleep-Consolidating-LLM"},{"id":"quantum-compilation","name":"Verifiable Quantum Circuit Compilation","kind":"Kaggle competition · quantum compilation","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"6.43×","label":"lower cost on invsqrt, own circuit (2,076,774 → 323,146)"},"stack":["Python","kickmix","Qualtran","NumPy","GF(2^12) tower fields","Kaggle"],"summary":"Fault-tolerant quantum algorithms are priced in qubits and Toffoli gates. Own circuits for two of the three tasks, built with kickmix and checked with the official verifier logic.","url":"#p-quantum-compilation","repo":"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-verifiable-quantum-circuit-compilation"},{"id":"santa-elf-calculator","name":"Santa 2026: Elf Calculator","kind":"Kaggle competition · program synthesis","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"128,494","label":"total instructions after merging public programs (sample: 1,490,379)"},"stack":["Python","C++","NumPy","pytest","Kaggle"],"summary":"For each of 1,000 puzzles a calculator with 48 registers starts from a few numbers and must produce every one of several huge targets (up to about 10^18) using + − × and exact division. An exact referee re-implements the rules and replays every program.","url":"#p-santa-elf-calculator","repo":"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/santa-2026"},{"id":"student-xai","name":"Explainable Student Performance","kind":"MLOps + explainability","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"9","label":"models benchmarked, explained with SHAP + LIME"},"stack":["scikit-learn","XGBoost","CatBoost","LightGBM","PyTorch","SHAP","LIME","MLflow","Docker"],"summary":"A model that predicts which students will struggle is only useful if teachers can see why it made each prediction. Pandera-validated data and a ColumnTransformer pipeline feed a benchmark of 9 models: linear, tree ensembles, XGBoost, CatBoost, LightGBM, a PyTorch MLP, a PyTorch LSTM and FLAML AutoML.","url":"#p-student-xai","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/student-performance-platform"},{"id":"dcgan","name":"DCGAN Reproducibility+","kind":"Generative vision research","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"5","label":"stability fixes over the 2015 paper, each documented"},"stack":["PyTorch","LSGAN","Spectral Norm","Matplotlib","Jupyter"],"summary":"The 2015 DCGAN is famously unstable, and most re-implementations show only a grid of blurry samples, never what the networks learned inside. Keeps Radford et al.'s all-convolutional architecture but swaps in an LSGAN loss, Spectral Normalization, a Xavier/Kaiming initialisation, discriminator dropout and label smoothing.","url":"#p-dcgan","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/Modern-DCGAN-Reproducibility"},{"id":"smaran","name":"Smaran","kind":"Windows desktop app","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"3.6.0","label":"current Windows release, installer and portable exe"},"stack":["Electron","React","TypeScript","Tailwind CSS","Zod","Vitest","Playwright","Ed25519"],"summary":"Reminder apps either shout at you or hide in a calendar, and Windows' Task Scheduler is too heavy for everyday routines. A tray app that parses plain sentences into schedules and slides in a small card with Done and Snooze.","url":"#p-smaran","repo":"https://info.sahaj.si/Smaran-Studio/"},{"id":"vmix-manager","name":"vMix Manager","kind":"Desktop production tool","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"XML","label":"edits .vmix session files directly, no running vMix needed"},"stack":["Python","PyQt6","XML","PyInstaller","vMix 24+"],"summary":"Large live shows have hundreds of playback files, and vMix's own interface means dragging them in one by one, renaming categories by hand and fixing broken paths input by input, hours before going on air. Instead of controlling vMix through its API, it edits the .vmix session file's XML directly, so nothing needs to be open.","url":"#p-vmix-manager","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/vMix-Manager"},{"id":"sentinel-proxy","name":"SentinelProxy Suite","kind":"Local network security tool","year":"2026","metric":{"value":"53 + 5309","label":"DNS and HTTPS-proxy ports served by one process"},"stack":["Node.js","dns2","http-proxy","SQLite3","Express","socket.io","node-forge"],"summary":"Ad and tracker blocking usually lives in a browser extension that malware and other apps can bypass, and DNS filters rarely show you what is happening live. A single Node.js process runs a DNS server on port 53 and an HTTPS proxy on port 5309.","url":"#p-sentinel-proxy","repo":"https://github.com/SahajIVVIX-1/SentinelProxy-Suite"}],"experience":[{"id":"hnnoix","role":"Research & Innovation Intern","org":"HNNOIX India Private Limited","kind":"Internship","when":"